# React components

Server Components by default. `"use client"` is a decision, not a habit — it is
what pulls code and dependencies into the browser bundle.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — whole page becomes client-side to get one interactive button
"use client";
export default function ProjectPage({ params }: Props) {
  const { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ["project"], queryFn: fetchProject });
  return <div>{data?.name}<ArchiveButton /></div>;
}

// ✅ GOOD — server renders, the boundary is as small as the interactivity
export default async function ProjectPage({ params }: Props) {
  const project = await getProject(params.id);
  return (
    <div>
      {project.name}
      <ArchiveButton projectId={project.id} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Responsibilities

- Components render state and raise events. No business rules, no `fetch` to your
  own API, no direct database access.
- Forms use React Hook Form with a Zod resolver, sharing the schema with the
  server. Client validation is UX; the server validates again because it must.
- Data mutations go through a Server Action or an oRPC mutation that calls an
  application service.

## Accessibility

Interactive elements are real elements: `button`, `a`, `label`-bound inputs. A
`div` with `onClick` is not keyboard reachable and fails the axe checks in CI.
Every image needs `alt`; decorative images take `alt=""`.
